/Title (Participants highlighted that intimate images, impersonating profiles, or manipulated content may circulate without the affected person immediately knowing. In such cases, friends, followers, or casual viewers may encounter the material first. However, existing systems place the burden of reporting primarily on the targeted individual. Participants therefore called for clearer mechanisms that allow bystanders to flag harmful content discreetly and responsibly, while ensuring the person affected is informed in a sensitive manner. They also expressed interest in making prior flagging visible, so others do not unknowingly amplify harmful material.)
